From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: meanaverage <aaronhritz@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 13:45:57 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] fix(connector): answer Auto-Detect Requests that come after
 the connect-time phase (#2128)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

## Summary

- A Windows Server 2022 RD Session Host with RDS licensing sends an
Auto-Detect Request (Bandwidth Measure Start, `SEC_AUTODETECT_REQ` on
the MCS message channel) in the middle of the licensing exchange,
between the platform challenge response and the new license, and again
while it prepares the Demand Active. The connector answers Auto-Detect
Requests only in its `ConnectTimeAutoDetection` phase, which the first
licensing PDU ends; every later phase hands whatever arrives to its own
decoder, so the licensing header's "invalid security header flags" ends
the connection before the session opens. This is #1629.
- The message channel is now told apart once, at the top of `step`, for
every phase that waits on the I/O channel, connect-time auto-detection
included. An Auto-Detect Request is answered through the existing
connect-time responder, which is what mstsc and FreeRDP do (FreeRDP's
licensing step notes the same of Windows Server 2012 R2 and 2019); one
that does not decode is logged and ignored, as the connect-time phase
always did (a server that waits for the reply then stalls until it times
out instead of failing fast, which the docs say); anything else on the
message channel is ignored; and the phase goes on waiting.
Multitransport bootstrapping hands anything without `SEC_TRANSPORT_REQ`
to the same handler, and an Initiate Multitransport Request outside its
phase is logged as a warning.
- This is the "answer and stay" shape discussed in #1629, applied once
ahead of every phase after the connect-time one rather than inside the
capabilities exchange alone. The log line for an answered request names
the phase.

## Scope

- Connection sequence only (`ironrdp-connector`). Auto-Detect Requests
after the session is up are #2012's, and this change is complementary to
it: with both, a host's probes are answered end to end.
- `ironrdp-pdu` is untouched: the parser is fine, the issue is which
PDUs get handed to it.
- Measurement results are the same nominal ones the connect-time phase
already returns (#1465); a real elapsed time is #1530's.

## Validation

- `cargo xtask check fmt typos lints tests locks` on the pinned
toolchain.
- Seven new connector tests
(`ironrdp-testsuite-core/tests/connector/autodetect.rs`): an Auto-Detect
Request in each of the licensing, multitransport bootstrapping,
capabilities exchange and connection finalization phases is answered and
the phase goes on; an unrelated message channel PDU is ignored; the PDU
from #1629's report, byte for byte. They fail without the change.
- A/B against a real host, a Windows Server 2022 Standard Evaluation
(10.0.20348) with the Remote Desktop Session Host and Remote Desktop
Licensing roles (Per Device, the license server unactivated, so it
issues temporary CALs; nothing bought). The host sends the request in
the middle of licensing on every connection. Without this change, 0 of 5
connections succeed, each with the error from #1629 (`[decode during
SERVER_NEW_LICENSE/LicenseExchangeState::UpgradeLicense] … invalid
security header flags`). With it, 10 of 10 connect, each log showing the
request answered after the platform challenge response, then
`ServerUpgradeLicense` and `License verified with success`, then the
session.
- The same against a Windows Server 2025 Standard Evaluation
(10.0.26100) host set up the same way: 5 of 5 connect with and without
the change. 2025 sends its first Auto-Detect Request only once the
session is up, so it never collides with licensing.

## Notes

- Which detection sends the colliding request: on the 2022 host, the
policy "Select network detection on the server" (`SelectNetworkDetect`)
set to turn off continuous detection (2) or both (3) stops every probe,
and an unpatched client then connects; turning off connect-time
detection alone (1) changes nothing. So it is continuous network
detection starting early, not the connect-time pass, which may help
explain why only some 2022 hosts show it.
- Found through meanaverage/tabby-rdp#106, whose reporter sees it on one
Windows Server 2022 host among many 2019–2022 ones; the others
presumably end licensing with `STATUS_VALID_CLIENT` and never open the
window.

Resolves #1629.

